= Consistency (logic) =

== Overview ==
'''Logical consistency''' is a property of a set of statements or formal system indicating that contradiction or triviality is not derivable under the relevant logic.

The exact formulation depends on the logic and proof system.

== Syntactic consistency ==
In a classical setting, a theory T is syntactically consistent if there is no formula φ such that:

<pre>
T ⊢ φ
and
T ⊢ ¬φ
</pre>

Equivalent formulations may define consistency by requiring that not every formula be derivable.

== Semantic consistency ==
A set of statements is satisfiable when there is a model in which they are all true.

Soundness guarantees that satisfiability implies syntactic consistency.

For classical first-order logic, completeness also connects consistency to satisfiability.

== Consistency is not truth ==
A consistent theory can still be false about the world.

Consistency only rules out a particular kind of internal logical failure.

Therefore:

<pre>
consistency ≠ truth
</pre>

== Consistency and completeness ==
Logical consistency is also distinct from completeness.

A consistent formal theory may leave some statements undecided.

Gödel's incompleteness theorems show strong limits on sufficiently expressive formal systems containing arithmetic.

== Relative consistency ==
A consistency proof usually relies on some background theory.

Showing that theory A has a model if theory B is consistent establishes relative consistency rather than an assumption-free guarantee.

